Delayed superficial wound healing and minor chemical irritation due to excessive application of iodine
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Persistent periwound erythema, Superficial epidermal sloughing, Yellow-brown skin discoloration

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-003
Reason for Visit: Delayed wound healing with new-onset persistent erythema and burning sensation concerning for developing local wound infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage highlighted persistent erythema extending beyond wound margins and epidermal sloughing. Urgent physical assessment was mandated to distinguish between chemical dermatitis and ascending bacterial cellulitis prior to the next in-hours period, avoiding potential systemic spread and the need for intravenous antibiotics.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The skin around my scrape is red, peeling, and burns when I put antiseptic on it.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports persistent erythema surrounding the wound margins beyond the acute injury phase. Notes localized pruritus over the iodine-treated area and superficial epidermal sloughing or dryness. Describes a mild burning sensation upon subsequent topical applications of iodine. Yellow-brown skin discoloration corresponding to the application site is evident. No purulent exudate. No systemic fever.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Over-treatment of minor injuries due to health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/80 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, focused on the wound appearance.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals a clean but non-granulating wound base. Notable for persistent periwound erythema, superficial epidermal sloughing, and yellow-brown skin discoloration strictly confined to the application area of iodine. Absence of purulent exudate. Lack of ascending lymphangitis. Normal systemic temperature.
Environmental Observations: Multiple over-the-counter antiseptic bottles visible on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient heavily using topical povidone-iodine against previous advice.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Delayed superficial wound healing and minor chemical irritation due to excessive application of iodine - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of ascending lymphangitis, normal systemic temperature, and erythema strictly confined to the chemical application zone.

Purulent Skin Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of purulent exudate and a clean wound base.

Fungal Skin Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute onset correlating directly with chemical application and lack of satellite lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of expanding periwound erythema and epidermal sloughing carries a high risk of mimicking aggressive bacterial cellulitis. This necessitated an urgent face-to-face home assessment to accurately diagnose chemical irritation versus infectious pathology. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period risked inappropriate administration of empirical antibiotics or unchecked progression of an actual infection. The prompt clinical evaluation ensured appropriate de-escalation of chemical trauma.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate cessation of topical iodine. Cleansing of the area with normal saline. Application of a sterile, inert protective dressing. Swab taken for routine culture.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wash the wound only with clean tap water and apply a simple dressing to allow natural cellular repair without chemical cytotoxicity.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of basic non-adherent dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to confirm resolution of erythema.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the redness rapidly expands beyond the current margins, red streaks appear moving up the limb, or systemic symptoms like fever and chills develop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes chemical irritation; wound swab results will definitively rule out occult bacterial colonization.
Clinical Course: Erythema and burning should subside within 48-72 hours after stopping the chemical irritant.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the wound begins to drain pus, smells foul, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Strong antiseptics like iodine kill bacteria but also destroy newly forming healthy skin cells, delaying healing and causing chemical burns when overused.
Actionable Steps: Stop using all antiseptics. Keep the wound covered and dry.

